<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Queen Victoria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Victoria of the United Kingdom · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Victoria was queen of the United Kingdom from 1837 to 1901 and empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1876. Her long reign saw industrial growth, political reform and the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. Her mother was a princess of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> whose first marriage had been to a prince of Leiningen. She chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> as the capital of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-victoria.jpg" alt="Queen Victoria" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Victoria_by_Bassano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 27 June 1890 Lewanika signed the Lochner Concession with the British South Africa Company, believing he was placing his country under the protection of <b>Queen Victoria</b>; in practice it granted the company mineral and commercial rights in return for an annual subsidy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 November 1858 Lord Canning, the first viceroy, read <b>Queen Victoria</b>’s proclamation at Allahabad, promising religious toleration, equal access to office and respect for the rights of the Indian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottawa was chosen as capital of the Province of Canada by <b>Queen Victoria</b> in 1857, partly as a compromise between the rival cities of Toronto, Kingston, Montreal and Quebec.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis IV married Princess Alice, daughter of <b>Queen Victoria</b>, and their daughters included Alix, who became Empress Alexandra of Russia in 1894, and Elisabeth, Grand Duchess Elizabeth Feodorovna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Queen Victoria</b> (r. 1837–1901) reigned as a constitutional monarch, and the Northcote–Trevelyan Report of 1854 led to a civil service recruited by competitive examination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Queen Victoria</b> was proclaimed Empress of India in 1877.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kensington Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Osborne House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.19&amp;year=1872" aria-label="Kensington Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4546 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Queen Victoria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4996 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4996 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.19&amp;year=1872">What was here in 1872? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Since it has pleased Providence to place me in this station, I shall do my utmost to fulfil my duty towards my country…&#8221;</p><footer>Journal, 20 June 1837, the day of her accession &#183; original &#183; from The Letters of Queen Victoria (1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20023/pg20023-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I wish you could have witnessed the 1st May 1851, the greatest day in our history, the most beautiful and imposing and touching spectacle ever seen, and the triumph of my beloved Albert.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to King Leopold on the Great Exhibition, 1851 &#183; original &#183; from The Letters of Queen Victoria (1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24780/pg24780-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The poor fatherless baby of eight months is now the utterly broken-hearted and crushed widow of forty-two! My life as a happy one is ended! the world is gone for me!&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to King Leopold after Prince Albert's death, December 1861 &#183; original &#183; from The Letters of Queen Victoria (1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28649/pg28649-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
